1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,400
Hello, I'm Hua Yan.

2
00:00:02,833 --> 00:00:07,600
I'm sorry I cannot join you in Strasbourg in
person, so this is a recorded presentation.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:15,033
In dynamic endoscopic reconstruction, performance tables help
us choose baselines and decide what to investigate next,

4
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:17,767
before anything reaches clinical evaluation.

5
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:23,433
But two results labelled masked PSNR may score different pixels.

6
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:27,100
Reproducing each number does not resolve that difference.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,133
Our paper asks when the available evidence
supports the intended comparison.

8
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:35,867
We first tested this question on our own archived results.

9
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:40,767
We asked three questions of seven archived results.

10
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:44,033
Reproduce: could we regenerate the scores?

11
00:00:44,767 --> 00:00:48,967
Five rows replayed, with all twenty tested
cells matching the recorded values.

12
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:53,567
Trace: could we identify the outputs
and their exact source record?

13
00:00:54,367 --> 00:00:58,767
Four replayable rows were bound to
an exact source record; one was not.

14
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:04,667
Compare: did the original scores support a
comparison of the two source-bound output sets?

15
00:01:05,133 --> 00:01:10,933
Their evaluation regions differed: the scores
replayed exactly, yet they were not yet comparable.

16
00:01:11,767 --> 00:01:15,133
The next step was to rescore the
saved outputs on a shared region.

17
00:01:17,467 --> 00:01:22,400
We kept the EndoGaussian and SurgicalGaussian
outputs fixed and changed the scoring region.

18
00:01:22,867 --> 00:01:29,900
On the common dataset-defined region, SurgicalGaussian's
outputs scored higher by about one point two seven decibels.

19
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:35,767
A further restriction to valid tissue reduced that
advantage to about zero point four five decibels.

20
00:01:36,267 --> 00:01:40,933
The aggregate direction stayed the same, but
the two scenes favoured different outputs.

21
00:01:41,267 --> 00:01:45,933
This is a comparison of two saved output
sets under one specified measurement.

22
00:01:46,333 --> 00:01:50,767
Comparing the methods themselves would also
need matched training and production evidence.

23
00:01:51,467 --> 00:01:54,633
This repair depended on having the outputs and scoring details.

24
00:01:57,333 --> 00:02:01,000
How much of that evidence could we recover
from publications and released source?

25
00:02:01,733 --> 00:02:06,800
We examined twenty-seven results from eight
papers within a predefined sixteen-paper corpus.

26
00:02:07,233 --> 00:02:10,067
The metric family was identifiable for every result.

27
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Yet the actual rule selecting evaluation
frames was fully specified for none.

28
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:19,633
Useful components were available, but key
measurement details remained unresolved.

29
00:02:20,567 --> 00:02:28,133
Until those details are supplied, a cross-paper comparison
stays open; the gaps do not show that any score is wrong.

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:36,633
Together, these studies show that before acting on a
result, we should first know what that result supports:

31
00:02:37,067 --> 00:02:43,967
compare saved outputs under a shared measurement, rescore
a repairable mismatch, or seek missing information.

32
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,467
I already use agents for algorithm and engineering development.

33
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:53,567
My long-term goal is to build research
agents that propose algorithmic changes,

34
00:02:53,867 --> 00:03:00,867
implement and run experiments, evaluate the results, and
choose the next experiment using verifiable evidence.

35
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:06,067
A next study could test whether these checks
improve those decisions under human supervision,

36
00:03:06,367 --> 00:03:08,433
including when to request human review.

37
00:03:09,067 --> 00:03:13,667
So when two scores share a metric name, ask
first whether they scored the same pixels.

38
00:03:14,533 --> 00:03:16,200
Questions are welcome by email.

39
00:03:16,633 --> 00:03:17,633
Thank you.
